type\nescalate_only\naudience\nL1 Support Agent (authorized admin)\nrequired_role\nCoreID: Password Reset Admin\norg\nSolandra Corp\napplication\nCoreID Admin Console (mock)\nPurpose\nWhen a user's self-service password reset fails or the user has no working verification channel, the ticket escalates to L1. An authorized L1 agent with the Password Reset Admin role in CoreID resets the password directly from the admin console.\nBecause the agent's own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and role-gated, this flow does not send a one-time verification code to the user. That code-based check exists to authenticate an otherwise-unverified self-service claimant; an admin acting under an authenticated, role-scoped session establishes identity through the caller checklist instead (Step 3).\nProcedure\nInputs\nOpen ticket with the requester's employee ID and manager name on file\nYour own authenticated CoreID session with the Password Reset Admin role assigned\nCaller identity verification checklist (minimum 2 of 4 items matched)\nSteps\nStep 1: Ticket reviewed\nNavigation: Ticketing system → open ticket → Requester Details panel\nConfirm the ticket category reads Password Reset — Escalated, and note the requester's employee ID and manager name. ⚠️ Do not proceed if the ticket is missing a manager name — request it from the requester first.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 1 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a ticketing system, specifically the \"Ticket #SOL-84213\" page. The page displays various details about the ticket, including the category, requester, employee ID, manager, department, and opened date.\n\n*   **Ticket Information**\n    *   Category: Password Reset - Escalated\n    *   Requester: J. Employee\n    *   Employee ID: SOL-04821\n    *   Manager: A. Menon\n    *   Department: Finance\n    *   Opened: Today, 10:42 AM\n*   **Error Messages**\n    *   None visible\n*   **Likely Issue Category and Severity**\n    *   Category: Software/Application\n    *   Severity: Medium\n    *   Reason: The ticket is related to a password reset, which is a common issue in software applications.\n*   **Observed Facts vs. Inference**\n    *   **Observed Facts**\n        *   The ticket number is SOL-84213.\n        *   The category is Password Reset - Escalated.\n        *   The requester is J. Employee.\n        *   The employee ID is SOL-04821.\n        *   The manager is A. Menon.\n        *   The department is Finance.\n        *   The ticket was opened today at 10:42 AM.\n    *   **Inference**\n        *   The issue is likely related to a password reset, as indicated by the category.\n        *   The employee ID and manager information suggest that the issue may be specific to a particular user or team.\n*   **Confidence Score and Notes**\n    *   Confidence Score: 0.95\n    *   Confidence Level: High\n    *   Confidence Reason: The image is clear and well-lit, making it easy to read the text and identify the relevant information. The layout is also straightforward, with clear headings and sections.\n\nStep 2: User located and lock confirmed\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → Users → Search bar\nSearch by employee ID. Confirm the account status badge shows Locked before continuing — do not reset a password on an account that isn't actually locked without further checks.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 2 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.80 / Medium]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a user signed in as A. Rao (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin). The user's name is displayed in the top-right corner of the screen.\n\n* **CoreID Admin Console**\n\t+ The CoreID Admin Console is displayed on the left side of the screen.\n\t+ It has a dark blue background with white text.\n\t+ The menu options are: Admin Console, Dashboard, Tickets, Users, Reports, and Settings.\n* **User Information**\n\t+ The user's name is displayed in the top-right corner of the screen.\n\t+ The user's name is A. Rao (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin).\n* **Users Tab**\n\t+ The Users tab is selected.\n\t+ The user's information is displayed below the tab.\n\t+ The user's ID is SOL-04821.\n\t+ The user's role is J. Employee.\n\t+ The user's department is Finance.\n* **Locked Button**\n\t+ A locked button is displayed next to the user's information.\n\t+ The button is red and has a white lock icon.\n\t+ The button is labeled \"Locked\".\n\nThe image shows that the user A. Rao (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin) is signed in to the CoreID Admin Console and has access to the Users tab. The user's information is displayed, including their ID, role, and department. A locked button is also displayed next to the user's information, indicating that the user's account is currently locked.\n\n**Extracted Text:**\n\n* None\n\n**Errors and Warnings:**\n\n* None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity:**\n\n* Category: Unknown\n* Severity: Unknown\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference:**\n\n* OBSERVED FACTS:\n\t+ The user A. Rao (L1 Support - Password Reset Admin) is signed in to the CoreID Admin Console.\n\t+ The user's information is displayed, including their ID, role, and department.\n\t+ A locked button is displayed next to the user's information.\n* INFERENCE:\n\t+ The user's account may be locked due to security reasons.\n\t+ The user may need to contact the administrator to unlock their account.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes:**\n\n* CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.8\n* CONFIDENCE LEVEL: Medium\n* CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear, but the text is not legible, and the context is limited.\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a white box in the center titled \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\". The box contains a list of options, including \"Employee ID matches ticket\", \"Manager name matches ticket\", and \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\". The first two options are checked, while the third option is unchecked. Below the list, there is a message that reads \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n\nHere are the main points from the image:\n\n* **CoreID Admin Console**: The top-left corner of the image shows the CoreID Admin Console logo and menu.\n* **White Box**: The white box in the center of the image contains the title \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\" and a list of options.\n* **Options**: The list includes:\n\t+ Employee ID matches ticket\n\t+ Manager name matches ticket\n\t+ Last 4 digits of phone on file\n* **Checked Options**: The first two options are checked, while the third option is unchecked.\n* **Message**: Below the list, there is a message that reads \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n\nOverall, the image appears to be a screenshot of an identity verification process for an employee, with the user having completed two out of four required steps.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a login page for an application called \"CoreID\" with a reset password form. The purpose of the image is to provide a visual representation of the login page and the reset password form.\n\n* A login page for an application called \"CoreID\"\n\t+ The login page has a dark blue background with white text.\n\t+ The top-left corner has a logo that says \"CoreID\" in white text.\n\t+ Below the logo, there is a navigation menu with options such as \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n\t+ In the center of the page, there is a large white box with a heading that says \"Reset Password - J. Employee\".\n\t+ Below the heading, there is a temporary password field with a checkbox next to it that says \"Force change at next sign-in\".\n\t+ There is a blue button below the checkbox that says \"Generate Temporary Password\".\n* A reset password form\n\t+ The reset password form is located below the login page.\n\t+ It has a white background with black text.\n\t+ The form has fields for entering a new password and confirming the new password.\n\t+ There is a blue button at the bottom of the form that says \"Reset Password\".\n* A navigation menu\n\t+ The navigation menu is located on the left side of the page.\n\t+ It has options such as \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n\t+ Each option has a small icon next to it.\n\nOverall, the image provides a clear visual representation of the login page and the reset password form for the CoreID application. The navigation menu on the left side of the page provides easy access to various features and settings.\n\nStep 5: Temporary password relayed securely\nNavigation: Phone / voice channel — not logged in CoreID\nRead the temporary password to the caller verbally over the verified phone line. Never send it by email, chat, or ticket comment in plain text. See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 5 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console is displayed on the left side of the image.\n    *   It has a dark blue background with white text.\n    *   The menu options are: Admin Console, Dashboard, Tickets, Users, Reports, and Settings.\n*   **Ticket #SOL-84213 - Resolution**\n    *   The ticket number is SOL-84213.\n    *   The status is Resolved.\n    *   The resolution notes are: Identity verified (2 of 4 checks). Temporary password issued via CoreID. Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled. No OTP sent - admin-mediated reset per policy.\n*   **Resolution notes**\n    *   The resolution notes are displayed in a text box below the ticket information.\n    *   They provide details about the resolution of the ticket, including the verification of identity and the issuance of a temporary password.\n\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes. The resolution notes provide details about the resolution of the ticket, including the verification of identity and the issuance of a temporary password.\n\nOutputs\nUser account unlocked with a valid temporary password\nForce-change-at-next-sign-in flag enabled\nTicket resolved with an audit note logged\nFAQs\nQ: What if the caller can't confirm at least 2 of the 4 identity items?\nDo not issue a reset. Escalate to your team lead, or ask the requester to retry self-service once they regain access to a verified phone or email.\nQ: Why doesn't this flow send a verification code the way self-service does?\nThe self-service code exists to authenticate an anonymous claimant with no other proof of identity. Here, identity is established two ways at once: your own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and scoped to the Password Reset Admin role, and the caller checklist independently confirms the requester is who they claim to be. Adding a code on top of that verifies nothing new — it would just be an unnecessary step to a channel the caller may not have working anyway, which is usually why the ticket was escalated in the first place.\n\n## LINKED REFERENCE KNOWLEDGE (Scraped from embedded SOP URLs):\n### Reference Web Page [https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords]:\nURL: https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords\nStatus Code: 200\n\n--- SCRAPED MARKDOWN CONTENT START ---\n[Skip to main content](#main)\n\n![U.S. flag](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/us_flag_small.png)\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere’s how you know\n\nHere’s how you know\n\n![Dot gov](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-dot-gov.svg)\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\n\n A **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n![HTTPS](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-https.svg)\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\n\n A **lock** (LockA locked padlock) or **https://** means you’ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n\n[Staying Secure at Events](/staying-secure-large-scale-events)[no-cost Cyber Services](/resources-tools/resources/free-cybersecurity-services-and-tools \"Free Cyber Services\")[secure your business](/secureyourbusiness)[Known Exploited